.TH XGPUPROFILE 1 "August 2026" "xgpuprofile VERSION" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
xgpuprofile \- choose which GPU drives the X display
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xgpuprofile
.RB [ \-\-status ]
.RB [ \-\-mode
.IR MODE ]
.RB [ \-\-once
.IR MODE ]
.RB [ \-\-refresh ]
.RB [ \-\-restart\-x ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-verbose ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
On most laptops with hybrid graphics the external video outputs are wired
to the discrete GPU. While the integrated GPU owns the X screen, high
refresh rate and resolution monitors lag. Handing X to the discrete GPU
resolves the problem.

.PP
The cost is that the discrete GPU can then never reaches its deepest idle
power state.

.B xgpuprofile
resolves this by starting the X screen on the appropriate GPU, based on
the current state of the laptop (AC power, external monitors).

.PP
Nothing is switched while X is running. Xorg fixes its GPU assignment when
the server starts, so a change is only ever
.I staged
\- written to the fragment \- and takes effect the next time Xorg starts.
No kernel modules are unloaded and no session is torn down behind your
back, which is where tools that switch live tend to hang or fail.

.SH MODES
.TP
.B hybrid
Keep the integrated GPU. The ordinary low\-power arrangement.
.TP
.B auto
Decide with the rule from the configuration \- see
.BR RULES .
.TP
.B dgpu
Always hand the display to the discrete GPU.

.SH ACTIONS
One at a time.
.B \-\-status
is used when none is given.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Print the saved mode, what was detected, and whether the running session is
using the layout the configuration now calls for.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " MODE"
Save
.I MODE
and stage the layout it implies. Persists across reboots.
.TP
.BI \-\-once " MODE"
Stage
.B dgpu
or
.B hybrid
for the next session only, ignoring the saved mode and rule, and write
nothing to the configuration file. Nothing has to be undone afterwards: the
next
.B \-\-refresh
decides again from the unchanged configuration, and the one that runs at
boot does so on its own.
.TP
.B \-\-refresh
Decide from the saved configuration and update the fragment. This is what
.B xgpuprofile.service
runs before Xorg starts; running it by hand is only needed to re\-stage
after the hardware or the configuration has changed mid\-session.
.TP
.B \-\-restart\-x
Restart the display manager so a staged change applies immediately. Ends
the current session, and asks for confirmation first.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Use a different configuration file.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
With
.BR \-\-refresh ,
report the decision and change nothing. Needs no privileges.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Explain each step on standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.

.SH RULES
With
.BR "mode = auto" ,
the
.B rule
setting decides when to use the discrete GPU.
.TP
.B both
On mains power and with an external display attached. The default.
.TP
.B either
On mains power or with an external display attached.
.TP
.B ac
On mains power, whatever is plugged in.
.TP
.B external
With an external display attached, whatever the power source.
.TP
.B always
Unconditionally, which is the same as
.BR "mode = dgpu" .
.PP
A machine with no battery counts as being on mains.

.SH DETECTION
External displays are counted from a live X session via
.BR xrandr (1)
when
.B DISPLAY
is reachable, since that reflects what is actually driving the desktop.
Kernel sysfs connector status is used only when no X session can be
reached, which is the case at boot, before Xorg starts.

.PP
The discrete GPU is the PCI display device the firmware did not mark
.IR boot_vga ,
and the integrated one is the device that carries that mark. The integrated
GPU stays in the generated layout as an
.B Inactive
device, because the built\-in panel is wired to it and leaving it out turns
the internal display off. Xorg BusIDs are emitted in decimal, though the
sysfs slot names are hexadecimal, and driver names come from the bound
kernel driver. All of these can be overridden in the configuration.

.PP
External displays are outputs whose name is not an internal panel type \-
eDP, LVDS, DSI. Excluding those matters: on a hybrid laptop the built\-in
panel is often reachable through the discrete GPU as well, and counting it
would leave the external\-display condition permanently true.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/xgpuprofile.conf
Configuration; every setting is documented in the file.
.TP
.I /etc/X11/xorg.conf.d/10-xgpuprofile.conf
The generated fragment. Written and removed automatically; edits are lost.

.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Follow the rule from now on:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo xgpuprofile \-\-mode auto
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use the discrete GPU for one session, with no monitor attached, without
disturbing the saved configuration:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo xgpuprofile \-\-once dgpu
sudo xgpuprofile \-\-restart\-x
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what would be chosen, changing nothing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xgpuprofile \-\-refresh \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR Xorg (1),
.BR xrandr (1)
